1: # P3K.35. Proved: the actual weighted shorter parameter
2: 
3: Status: Proved after the root's complete six-section audit and the
4: independent checks audit. This statement concerns the whole-zero I1 families of
5: the canonical fixed-source catalogue, not the component-supported OS49
6: completion or loops varying the source surface.
7: 
8: ## 1. Scope and the actual map
9: 
10: Write the actual source line as
11: \(\mathcal M=\mathcal O(aG-O+D)\), with the prescribed vertical
12: correction when it occurs, and the contracting source translation as
13: \(-bG\). The whole-zero catalogue has \(ab=d\), where \(h(G)=1/d\).
14: Let the actual nontrivial cap orders be \(m_i\), put
15: \(M=\prod_i m_i\), and include any order-one caps in the signed
16: norm equation. The sphere datum is
17: \[
18:            \sum_i P_i=\varepsilon,\qquad
19:            P_i=\frac{M n_i}{m_i},\qquad \varepsilon=\pm1.
20:                                                                \tag{WS.1}
21: \]
22: This sum includes K32's actual ordinary framing chart. If its marked
23: base relation is \(\sum\alpha_i+b_0\gamma=0\), that chart has
24: transition \(h=\gamma+b_0\alpha\), effective order one, norm
25: \(-b_0\), and \(P_{\rm frame}=-Mb_0\). Thus (WS.1) is exactly
26: \[
27:  m_0n_\infty+m_\infty n_0
28:                 +M\left(\sum_jn_j-b_0\right)=\varepsilon.
29: \]
30: The framing chart is the actual base-atlas map of K32, with no
31: additional elliptic or line clutch. Its exponent is retained in
32: the closing rational function below.
33: For the already constructed one-gamma family the parameter is
34: \(\lambda=r^M\), and the actual finite-root maps satisfy
35: \[
36:  A_{i,r}=r^{P_i}A_{i,0},\qquad B_\lambda=r^M B_0,
37:  \qquad A_{i,0}^{m_i}=B_0^{n_i},\qquad[A_{i,0},B_0]=1.
38:                                                                \tag{WS.2}
39: \]
40: Every weighted row has \(b\mid M\). The construction below gives
41: an actual free deck action over \(r\mapsto\zeta_b r\), with all
42: cap, line, and cusp maps retained. It descends the family to
43: \[
44:                      w=r^b,\qquad \lambda=w^{M/b}.       \tag{WS.3}
45: \]
46: The single positive \(w\)-loop has a closed simultaneous Cox product.
47: Put \(H=Ma^2/d\) and let \(\chi\) be the page holomorphic Euler
48: characteristic from K30/K32. Its integers and index are
49: \[
50:  k_w=-\frac{3H}{b},\qquad
51:  \ell_w=-\frac{24\chi}{b},\qquad
52:  A_w=\frac{\chi-H/2}{b}.                              \tag{WS.4}
53: \]
54: These are characteristic-number equalities on an explicitly descended
55: family. For a fan with primitive physical cocharacter norm
56: \(S=p^2-pq+q^2\), K31 then gives
57: \[
58:  [\Sigma_{\Phi_w,T}]
59:       =A_w+\frac{\ell_w}{8}(S-1)
60:                    -\frac{k_w}{8}(S^2-1)\pmod {28}.     \tag{WS.5}
61: \]
62: No division of a homotopy-sphere class is used.
63: 
64: ## 2. The meromorphic source lift and its actual norm
65: 
66: The generic-fibre source translation by \(+G\) fixes every degree-zero
67: Picard class. Here its isomorphism is literal. Put \(P=aG\) and
68: \(D_P=P-O\). For \(a\ne1\), take the ratio of the two chord functions
69: through \((P-G,O)\) and \((P,-G)\). Their third intersection is the
70: same, and the ratio \(f\) has divisor
71: \[
72:              \operatorname{div}(f)
73:                  =(P-G)+O-P-(-G)=t_G^*D_P-D_P.         \tag{WS.6}
74: \]
75: For \(a=1\), the exact function is \(f=1/(x-x(G))\), with the
76: same divisor. In the rational section frame, the isomorphism
77: \(t_G^*\mathcal M\to\mathcal M\) sends \(t_G^*s\) to \(fs\);
78: its inverse supplies a meromorphic bundle map \(U\) covering \(+G\).
79: The prescribed vertical divisor \(D\) is retained in its rational
80: frame. Its zeros and poles are vertical, because (WS.6) is already
81: an isomorphism on every generic fibre.
82: 
83: The actual map \(B_0^{-1}\) covers \(+bG\). Thus
84: \[
85:                         \rho(t)=B_0^{-1}/U^b            \tag{WS.7}
86: \]
87: is a meromorphic base function. To verify this assertion, compare the
88: two line maps on a generic compact elliptic fibre: their ratio has
89: neither a zero nor a pole there and is therefore constant. The resulting
90: function lies in the rational function field of the base, because both
91: maps are the displayed rational divisor maps. At the selected I1, if
92: the divisor order of \(U\) is \(e\), then
93: \[
94:                       \operatorname{ord}\rho=-1-be.     \tag{WS.8}
95: \]
96: The \(-1\) is the actual simple pole of the inverse of the given
97: linearization section. At an uncapped retained fibre the line has zero
98: degree on every component. The vertical divisor of \(U\) has zero
99: intersection with every component, so it is an integral whole-fibre
100: multiple; the same local order is then \(-be\). This is the integral
101: kernel calculation already proved for the actual reduced fibres in
102: K14/K15. A local whole-fibre power therefore makes the lift invertible
103: there, and introduces no fractional source-root character.
104: 
105: This retains every vertical line in F52, not only the chosen
106: representative divisor. Its canonical rational section gives the
107: actual ordinary comparison. On a cap root F52's direct-image
108: evaluation gives \(N_i\simeq N_{i,0}\otimes f_i^*L_i^{\rm base}\),
109: after dividing by the displayed integral whole-fibre monomial.
110: Translation by \(G_i'\) fixes this base line. Any extra deck
111: character multiplies every fibre weight by the same scalar and
112: therefore cancels in the commutator ratios below. At the selected
113: zero a coefficient \(kF\) has the literal frame map
114: \((Z,V,q)\mapsto(Z,q^kV,q)\); its height-one lattice map translates
115: every vertex by \(k\delta\). This is the actual period reindexing
116: of F52, so it preserves all edge differences, the physical
117: cocharacter, and the integer \(S\) in the transported fan.
118: 
119: ## 3. Actual extending lifts on the cap root lines
120: 
121: Use the actual minimal root source \(S_i'\), its deck \(D_i\), and
122: the line \(N_i\) established by the source-line diagrams of F41--F49.
123: Its generic Picard point is \(aG_i'\). The source translation by
124: \(+G_i'\) extends on that actual minimal regular source. The generic
125: chord lift of (WS.6) differs from an everywhere invertible line map
126: only by a whole-fibre divisor: on a smooth root this follows from
127: the single reduced fibre, and on a reduced III, IV, II or I2 root
128: it follows from the zero component degrees and the primitive integral
129: kernel of its component intersection matrix. Remove that integer
130: base power. Denote the resulting extending map by \(L_i\).
131: 
132: All translation lifts on a degree-zero elliptic line commute. This
133: has an explicit generic-fibre model: write that line as
134: \((\mathbb C\times\mathbb C^*)/\Lambda\), with lattice action
135: \((z,v)\mapsto(z+\omega,\chi(\omega)v)\). The lifts are translations
136: in the first coordinate and scalar multiplications in the second,
137: which commute. Equality on the generic fibres extends over the actual
138: root line. In particular \(L_i\) commutes with the translation lift
139: \(F_i\) used in the cap's \(A_{i,0}\).
140: 
141: The deck commutator is a base unit:
142: \[
143:                       D_iL_iD_i^{-1}=\chi_i(z)L_i.
144: \]
145: Its cyclic norm is one. Divide by its constant value at zero, take
146: the logarithm \(g\) vanishing at zero, and put
147: \(\mathcal D f(z)=f(\zeta_{m_i}^{-1}z)\). The norm identity
148: gives \(\sum_j\mathcal D^jg=0\). The explicit function
149: \[
150:  h=-\frac1{m_i}\sum_{j=0}^{m_i-1}j\mathcal D^jg
151:  \quad\text{satisfies}\quad (\mathcal D-I)h=-g.
152: \]
153: Rescaling \(L_i\) by the target scalar \(e^h\) therefore makes
154: the commutator the constant
155: \(\chi_i=\chi_i(0)\). Once \(\chi_i^b=1\), the unit
156: \(L_i^b/B_0^{-1}\) is deck invariant. An invariant analytic b-th
157: root rescales \(L_i\) further so that
158: \[
159:  L_i^b=B_0^{-1},\qquad
160:  D_iL_iD_i^{-1}=\chi_i L_i,
161:  \qquad L_iA_{i,0}L_i^{-1}=\chi_i^{-1}A_{i,0}.          \tag{WS.9}
162: \]
163: The last equality uses the actual form \(A_{i,0}=\text{unit}\cdot
164: F_i^{n_i}D_i\) and the just-proved commutativity, including its
165: retained finite line character.
166: 
167: ### The good-root characters
168: 
169: On a good root let \(\rho_i\) be the deck derivative at a marked
170: fixed point of the central elliptic curve. The natural line
171: \(N_i=\mathcal O(aG_i'-O)\) has fibre weights \(\rho_i\) at
172: \(aG_i'\), \(\rho_i^{-1}\) at \(O\), and one at every other
173: fixed point. This is precisely the Cartier support-function line
174: map in F41/F42, including its exceptional correction, so it is
175: the actual cap line.
176: 
177: Translation by \(G_i'\) cycles the marked fixed points. Comparing
178: the fibre weights at two successive points gives its deck
179: commutator. For a good III or III* root the marked point has order
180: two, \(a\) is odd, and \(\rho_i\) has order four. The two successive
181: ratios are both \(\rho_i^2=-1\). For a good IV or IV* root the
182: marked point has order three. The three successive ratios are
183: all \(\rho_i^{-a}\). Thus
184: \[
185:  \chi_{\mathrm{III},\mathrm{III}^*}=-1,\qquad
186:  \chi_{\mathrm{IV},\mathrm{IV}^*}=\rho_i^{-a}.           \tag{WS.10}
187: \]
188: The actual minimal-root substitutions give
189: \(\rho_{\mathrm{IV}^*}=\zeta_3^2\) and
190: \(\rho_{\mathrm{IV}}=\zeta_3\). Consequently the single-cap
191: weighted E6 row \((a,b)=(2,3)\) and the ordinary IV row
192: \((a,b)=(10,3)\) both have \(\chi=\zeta_3^2\).
193: In the original E6 two-cap row \((1,6)\), the IV* character is
194: \(\zeta_3\) and the III character is \(-1\).
195: 
196: ### Reduced partners with the component class killed by a
197: 
198: In the weighted two-cap rows, a reduced cap is the partner on
199: which \(aG_i'\) has zero component class. Its central line is
200: trivial. One can check this on the actual subsequent good root:
201: after multiplication by \(a\), the marked section has reached
202: the identity point in the original minimal good model. Its
203: formal parameter vanishes to positive order. Substitution of a
204: root order \(m\ge2\) multiplies that order by \(m\). A nonzero
205: point in the additive identity group of a reduced II, III or IV
206: fibre has order exactly one in this good-root formal parameter,
207: as follows from \(x=w^2X,y=w^3Y\) and
208: \(-X/Y=w(-x/y)\). Therefore the root section specializes to
209: the zero element of the additive group. For the order-one root
210: the deck itself is the identity. In every case the central line
211: is trivial, or the deck is identity. A nonvanishing global
212: section of a trivial line on the connected proper central fibre
213: has a single deck eigencharacter, so comparison after translation
214: gives
215: \[
216:                               \chi_i=1.                 \tag{WS.11}
217: \]
218: This covers the reduced III partner of the E6 \((2,3)\) row,
219: the reduced IV partner of its \((3,2)\) row, the reduced II
220: partner of the E7 \((1,2)\) row, and the reduced IV partner
221: of the starred \((6,2)\) row.
222: 
223: ### The quadratic I1* root
224: 
225: Here retain the actual Tate marking
226: \[
227:  q_T=z^2u(z^2),\qquad Z_G=zv(z),\qquad
228:  v(z)v(-z)=-u(z^2).
229: \]
230: Thus \(+G\) exchanges the two central I2 components, whereas
231: the root deck fixes their labels and reverses each normalized
232: component. The actual root line is \(\mathcal O(aG'-O)\),
233: \(a=2\) or \(6\), with identity-component Picard parameter
234: \(Z_{aG'}(0)=-1\), by the exact F36/F38 line diagram.
235: 
236: Trivialize this multidegree-zero line on the two normalizations.
237: Its node-gluing scalars can be chosen \((\lambda_0,\lambda_1)
238: =(1,-1)\). Label the node from the infinity of \(C_j\) to
239: the zero of \(C_{j+1}\) by \(j\), with indices modulo two.
240: The translation exchanges the nodes in their cyclic order;
241: the deck exchanges their ends. If their frame factors are
242: \(t_j,d_j\), compatibility is literally
243: \[
244:  t_{j+1}\lambda_j=\lambda_{j+1}t_j,\qquad
245:  d_j=\lambda_j\lambda_{j+1}d_{j+1}.
246: \]
247: Hence \(t_1=-t_0\) and \(d_1=-d_0\). Their commutator is
248: \(d_{j+1}/d_j=-1\), independently of the nonzero coordinate
249: units. This proves on the actual corrected root line
250: \[
251:                          \chi_{I_1^*}=-1.              \tag{WS.12}
252: \]
253: It retains both components and both node gluings.
254: 
255: All these calculations retain arbitrary marked source coefficients.
256: F51 supplies the exact minimal Weierstrass root substitutions,
257: the linearization fixing the two marked sections, and the same
258: Cartier corrections with their holomorphic unit coefficients.
259: For the starred quadratic root it supplies precisely the units
260: \(u,v\) and the identity displayed above. Thus the character
261: calculation takes place on each actual root line under the F51/F52
262: maps, rather than identifying source members only by their fibre
263: profiles.
264: 
265: ## 4. The global rational root and exact finite-cap map
266: 
267: Pull the global meromorphic \(U\) to a root line by its already
268: proved rational line correspondence. Comparison with \(L_i\)
269: is a meromorphic scalar constant along generic elliptic fibres,
270: so it has the form
271: \[
272:                           U=z^{e_i}h_i(z)L_i
273: \]
274: with \(e_i\in\mathbb Z\) and \(h_i\) a nonzero holomorphic
275: unit after shrinking the disc. Since \(U\) is defined over
276: the original base, it is invariant under the deck conjugation.
277: For a target scalar the exact rule is
278: \[
279:  D_i m_f D_i^{-1}=m_{f(\zeta_{m_i}^{-1}z)}.
280: \]
281: Inserting the displayed comparison gives
282: \[
283:  \chi_i=\zeta_{m_i}^{e_i},\qquad
284:  h_i(\zeta_{m_i}z)=h_i(z),\qquad
285:  \operatorname{ord}_{q_i}\rho=-\frac{be_i}{m_i}.        \tag{WS.13}
286: \]
287: The last number is an integer, because \(\rho\) is a base
288: rational function. Write \(\chi_i=\zeta_b^{z_i}\); then
289: \(\operatorname{ord}_{q_i}\rho\equiv-z_i\pmod b\).
290: 
291: The actual one-gamma positive closing multiplier is the base
292: rational function \(h_0(t)=e^{2\pi iH(t)}\), with order
293: \(-P_i\) at cap \(i\), order \(\varepsilon\) at the
294: selected I1, and no other zeros or poles.
295: In an affine chart containing the finite sites it is explicitly
296: \[
297:  h_0(t)=c_0(t-t_c)^\varepsilon
298:                    \prod_{i\text{ finite}}(t-t_i)^{-P_i},
299:  \qquad c_0\ne0,
300: \]
301: with its recorded infinity order. The framing chart contributes
302: \((t-t_{\rm frame})^{Mb_0}\), exactly K32's inverse-closing
303: exponent with its arrow reversed. The constant is the fixed
304: bounded unit of the actual closing map. Therefore
305: \[
306:                 h_0(t)\rho(t)^\varepsilon
307: \]
308: has a divisor divisible by \(b\) exactly as follows. At the
309: selected I1, (WS.8) cancels the order \(\varepsilon\).
310: At any retained fibre its order is a multiple of \(b\).
311: At a cap its order modulo \(b\) is
312: \(-P_i-\varepsilon z_i\). The following actual character
313: table and the sphere equation prove this is zero in every row:
314: 
315: | Weighted sector | b | Nontrivial root orders | Character exponents z_i modulo b |
316: |---|---|---|---|
317: | E7 good III*, one cap | 2 | 4l | 1 |
318: | E6 good IV*, one cap | 3 | 3l | 2 |
319: | OS47 good III | 2 | 4l | 1 |
320: | OS56 good III | 2 | 4l | 1 |
321: | OS56 good IV | 3 | 3l | 2 |
322: | quadratic I1*, one cap | 2 | 2 | 1 |
323: | E6, (a,b)=(1,6) | 6 | 3l,4k | 2,3 |
324: | E6, (a,b)=(2,3) | 3 | 3l,4k+1 | 2,0 |
325: | E6, (a,b)=(3,2) | 2 | 3l+2,4k | 0,1 |
326: | E7, (a,b)=(1,2) | 2 | 4l,6k+1 | 1,0 |
327: | I1*+IV, (a,b)=(6,2) | 2 | 2,6l+1 | 1,0 |
328: | I1*+IV, (a,b)=(2,6) | 6 | 2,3k, k odd | 3,2 |
329: 
330: All root orders retain the catalogue's positivity and primitive
331: sphere restrictions. For one cap, \(P_i\equiv\varepsilon\pmod b\)
332: and the character is \(\zeta_b^{-1}\). For a two-cap row
333: the condition is the reduction of
334: \(m_jn_i+m_in_j=\varepsilon\) modulo two and/or three.
335: For example in the last row, \(3kn_*+2n_4=\varepsilon\)
336: gives \(3kn_*+3\varepsilon\equiv0\pmod6\) and
337: \(2n_4+2\varepsilon\equiv0\pmod6\). The other rows have
338: the identical displayed reductions. Every order-one cap has
339: \(\chi_i=1\) and \(P_i=Mn_i\), divisible by \(b\), so it
340: does not change these reductions. Their total contribution to
341: the sphere equation is also divisible by \(b\).
342: 
343: A rational function on \(\mathbb P^1\) whose divisor is
344: divisible by \(b\) has a rational b-th root: factor its zeros
345: and poles, divide their integer orders by \(b\), and choose a
346: b-th root of its nonzero constant. Choose this root \(g_0\)
347: with the specified cusp value. Then the actual ordinary endpoint
348: map is
349: \[
350:                     D_r=r^{-\varepsilon M/b}g_0(t)U^\varepsilon.
